    "body": "For terminals (such as linux without graphical environment) it poses an issue to default $TERM to \"xterm-256color\". Take as an example EDITOR being set to \"nvim\", and neovim is used to edit some file in a git repository. Afterwards to commit the changes you enter a terminal using \":terminal\", when you perform \"git commit\" the buffer won't display any colours due to neovim now being opened in the terminal with 'termguicolors' set (when it previously wasn't).\r\n\r\nThis patch resolves this issue, and should more accurately reflect the actual capabilities of the terminal to jobs started with \"jobstart\".\r\n\r\nAnother possibility is to feed-through $TERM, either on both cases or just for the 'notermguicolors' case. For this you'd need to still default to these if $TERM isn't set for the case of neovim being executed outside of a terminal emulator.\r\n\r\nThis may have unforeseen consequences due to ASCII escape sequences, being omitted by other programmes or different parts of neovim itself due to the absence of \"xterm-256color\".\r\nThis is less of an issue for the 'notermguicolors' case and in some cases may even be preferred.",
    "closed_at": "2026-08-11T22:53:22Z",
    "comment_data": [],
    "comment_regular_data": [
        {
            "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
            "body": "Is this true? I don't think xterm-256color defaults to true color because the terminfo lacks Tc, RGB, setrgbf, and setrgbb. I tried the steps and still got colors in a linux VT.",
