        {
            "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
            "body": "Sorry, I forgot to start nvim with `--clean` on the inner nvim when I tested, and I have `termguicolors = false` in my config. I see it now. Even though xterm-256color doesn't have anything for 24 bit color in its terminfo, `termguicolors` ends up true because of the SGR test in `defaults.lua` (and the embedded terminal does support 24 bit color). You can disable this automatic check with `NVIM_NOTTYFAST=true` to keep `termguicolors` from being set to true. You could add this to your nvim config (or shell, if you prefer) to set it conditionally:\r\n```lua\r\nif vim.fn.getenv('TERM') == \"linux\" then\r\n    vim.fn.setenv('NVIM_NOTTYFAST', 'true')\r\nend\r\n```\r\nI tried setting TERM to ansi, but it didn't seem to fix the color problem, while disabling termguicolors did. Maybe I'm doing something wrong.\r\n```bash\r\n# this has color in linux VT\r\nnvim --clean --cmd 'term nvim --clean --cmd \"set notermguicolors\"'\r\n# this doesn't\r\nnvim --clean --cmd 'term TERM=ansi nvim --clean'\r\n```\r\nI think the `TERM` on jobstart is supposed to represent the capabilities of nvim's virtual terminal, not the outer one. I found some other oddities with the colors when I was testing various configurations, but maybe that will change with libghostty.",
